        "title": "Welcome",
        "type": "start",
        "splitPosition": "split",
        "splitAnchor": 1.02,
        "turnByTurn": 0,
        "textContents": [
          {
            "pos": 2,
            "template": "plain",
            "body": "<p>Welcome to the tour of Dornbusch, including the Henry and Emma Budge retire­ment home, the Youth House and a home for sin­gle women!</p>\n<p><br />During the Weimar Republic, Frankfurt expe­ri­en­ced a severe hou­sing shor­tage, which aut­hori­ties sought to remedy by building numerous hou­sing esta­tes. These were primarily designed for families, but other social groups also nee­ded hou­sing and pla­ces for social exch­ange that met their needs. Private founda­ti­ons, asso­cia­ti­ons, and other initia­tives played a decisive role in the imple­men­ta­tion of these pro­je­cts. </p>\n<p><br />The approximately 40-min­ute tour through the Dornbusch district pres­ents three spe­cial types of building structu­res: a retire­ment home, a youth centre, and a block of flats for unmarried women. They were all built around 1930 on lar­gely undeve­l­o­ped urban land. Agricultu­ral use, such as the Grünhof, and the first blocks of flats on Rai­mund­strasse and Eschers­hei­mer Land­strasse collided here.</p>\n<p><br />We begin the tour with the for­mer Henry and Emma Budge retire­ment home. It is located behind a gar­den fence that shields it from the street. The Edin­ger­weg, to the left of the pro­perty, leads around the complex, allo­wing you to view it from ano­ther angle.</p>",
